10
00:00:51.229 --> 00:00:54.829
Now, I can use this, and you can see that I've got my checkerboard and everything else is showing up.

11
00:00:54.829 --> 00:01:01.429
But if I switch back and forth here, I've got a really easy view of my Nodal Point calibration toolset.

12
00:01:01.429 --> 00:01:05.829
Now, before I use this lens file, I actually want to duplicate this.

13
00:01:05.829 --> 00:01:11.530
So let's go ahead and connect to source control, and let's make a copy of our lens file.

14
00:01:11.530 --> 00:01:17.829
Because we've done our lens distortion mapping, and if we want to make any kind of adjustment with our Nodal Point for another camera combination,

15
00:01:17.829 --> 00:01:23.430
it's easier to use the lens distortion map than it is to make a brand new one from scratch.

16
00:01:23.430 --> 00:01:30.430
So again, from here, let's right-click and duplicate, and I'm going to call this one P3NP for Nodal Point.

17
00:01:30.430 --> 00:01:34.629
Now, that means that I'm going to actually need to connect a few other things here.

18
00:01:34.629 --> 00:01:40.430
So let's move this down and select my NDC and my camera, and let's look at our details panel,

19
00:01:40.430 --> 00:01:47.930
and move to our Zeiss Phys data, and we need to make the adjustment so that each of these is actually going to be using our Nodal Point.

20
00:01:47.930 --> 00:01:53.329
And let's make sure that my lens is using the same lens file by selecting that as well.

21
00:01:53.829 --> 00:01:56.829
Now, let's go ahead and mark this for add, just so that we have it active.

22
00:01:56.829 --> 00:02:01.329
And as we're getting ready to do this, I can go back to my Phys information,

23
00:02:01.329 --> 00:02:06.829
and scroll back down to choose my lens file, and make sure that we're actually using the lens file that is active in here.

24
00:02:06.829 --> 00:02:08.830
And I'm going to fill this up with my full screen.

25
00:02:08.830 --> 00:02:15.330
You can see that I've got my position with my focus, my lens, and my distortion parameters are still existing here as well.

26
00:02:15.330 --> 00:02:20.330
Now, I'm going to go into my Nodal Offset tab, because that's the calibration tool that I'm going to use.

27
00:02:20.330 --> 00:02:23.330
I'm going to use my Nodal Offset Points method.

28
00:02:23.330 --> 00:02:26.330
Now, I have multiple values in here, and I'm going to choose my calibrator.

29
00:02:26.330 --> 00:02:29.330
Now, the calibrator is the one that we built in the previous section.

30
00:02:29.330 --> 00:02:31.830
This is our Optipod Track calibrator.

31
00:02:31.830 --> 00:02:37.830
So if I select that, you can see I've got multiple values for my calibrator component, and a calibration point that's in here.

32
00:02:37.830 --> 00:02:41.330
Now, I want to be able to see what's happening, and I don't want any kind of distraction,

33
00:02:41.330 --> 00:02:44.330
so I'm going to make sure that this is fully transparent to see my object.

34
00:02:44.330 --> 00:02:47.330
And to get started, it works just the same as we did before.

35
00:02:47.330 --> 00:02:52.330
It allows me to make sure that my static mesh actor is correctly chosen, and I can see my calibrator.

36
00:02:52.330 --> 00:02:58.330
And now I'm going to have Sean come in and help me move this around after I do my first set of calibration points.

37
00:02:58.330 --> 00:03:01.330
Now, my first set of calibration points should automatically change.

38
00:03:01.330 --> 00:03:04.330
This says calibration point bottom left.

39
00:03:04.330 --> 00:03:08.330
It is the left calibration point of the actual object that we're going to be choosing.

40
00:03:08.330 --> 00:03:14.330
So you can see right over here, I can zoom in just using my middle mouse scroll wheel to do that.

41
00:03:14.330 --> 00:03:17.330
And as I scroll through, I get zoomed in.

42
00:03:17.330 --> 00:03:20.330
I want to get pretty close to the middle in here for my calibration point.

43
00:03:20.330 --> 00:03:25.650
And again, this is the left side of the object, not the left side of the screen.

44
00:03:25.650 --> 00:03:30.650
Now, as I do that, there's my first point, and you can see that changes to my bottom right, so I'm going to hit that next point.

45
00:03:30.650 --> 00:03:32.650
Then I'm going to do the same thing for my center.

46
00:03:32.650 --> 00:03:37.650
And as I mentioned before, I usually put a sticker on the puck so that I can know where the center of my object is.

47
00:03:37.650 --> 00:03:43.650
Then I move up here to my top left, and again, it's the left side of the actual calibration object.

48
00:03:43.650 --> 00:03:48.650
And I'm going to do it again over here for my final one, which is my top right.

49
00:03:48.650 --> 00:03:51.719
Now, you can see that this cycled back through for the calibration point.

50
00:03:51.719 --> 00:03:57.879
And if I move back, I want to move my puck on the stand around in order to get new points.

51
00:03:57.879 --> 00:04:03.069
Now, as you go through and move this, Sean's just going to pick any position that's within the frame.

52
00:04:03.069 --> 00:04:06.069
Now, with that in place, I can just kind of move up a little bit.

53
00:04:06.069 --> 00:04:09.069
I want to make sure that I'm seeing everything, so I want to scroll a little bit higher in there.

54
00:04:09.069 --> 00:04:18.069
And you can see, again, I'm choosing my bottom left, and I'm going to choose my bottom right, and I'm going to choose my center, and my top left, and my top right.

55
00:04:18.069 --> 00:04:23.069
And again, if you're wondering how I know which one, it's actually telling me which calibration point that's in here to choose.

56
00:04:23.069 --> 00:04:26.069
Now, I need Sean to move it again to the opposite side.

57
00:04:26.069 --> 00:04:31.069
These positions can move around. I need eight solid points, or different positions, in front of the camera.

58
00:04:31.069 --> 00:04:34.069
And he's got it set up pretty good right now, actually.

59
00:04:34.069 --> 00:04:38.069
He doesn't need to rotate it at all, because I can see clearly the four points that are in here.

60
00:04:38.069 --> 00:04:41.069
Now I'm going to scroll just a little bit to the top so I can get a better view.

61
00:04:41.069 --> 00:04:43.069
And again, I'm going to create my points.

62
00:04:43.069 --> 00:04:50.279
I'm going to have Sean move it again, and he can lower it down a little bit if he would like, or as low as we can possibly get it.

63
00:04:50.279 --> 00:04:54.079
There. That's kind of what we're looking at for our calibration.

64
00:04:54.079 --> 00:04:57.079
So let's zoom in a little bit, and again, let's move through our points.

65
00:04:57.079 --> 00:05:02.370
Now, as I finish up these points, this is the fourth position that we've actually done.

66
00:05:02.370 --> 00:05:05.370
You can see here, if I look through my list, there's my first set.

67
00:05:05.370 --> 00:05:09.370
And if I scroll down to the bottom, from my bottom left, there's one, two, three, four sections.

68
00:05:09.370 --> 00:05:12.370
So I've got four bottom lefts already, that's four positions.

69
00:05:12.370 --> 00:05:13.370
I need four more.

70
00:05:13.370 --> 00:05:17.370
Now, rather than keep you here for this, we're going to go ahead and skip ahead.

71
00:05:17.370 --> 00:05:25.199
Okay, so now that we've finished our calibration, we need to apply our calibration to the nodal offset that's in here.

72
00:05:25.199 --> 00:05:29.199
So, before we do, we want to be able to see real time what's going on.

73
00:05:29.199 --> 00:05:37.199
So I'm going to switch back over here to my Tesla remote, and I'm going to change back from my pattern to what's visible on the stage.

74
00:05:38.199 --> 00:05:41.199
So you can see now that the blue that was here is back to the stage.

75
00:05:41.199 --> 00:05:46.199
The reason for that is so that I can actually adjust my transparency here and see where my puck is located.

76
00:05:46.199 --> 00:05:51.199
Now, my lime green puck here, you can see it's not quite aligned with the marker.

77
00:05:51.199 --> 00:05:56.199
So if I zoom in here by scrolling with my middle mouse wheel, I just want to make sure that it's clear.

78
00:05:56.199 --> 00:06:01.199
You can see, here's my puck's alignment versus the digital CG object that's in here.

79
00:06:01.199 --> 00:06:10.199
Now, we are ready to apply this, and in order to do that, we just stay in this little window and we move right over to this add nodal offset calibration.

80
00:06:10.199 --> 00:06:16.199
Now, as you're ready to go with this, you just click on this button and all of our points here will get added.

81
00:06:16.199 --> 00:06:21.199
Now, I have more points here than just the eight points that you collect.

82
00:06:21.199 --> 00:06:28.199
I actually would recommend that you collect five to eight points at a specific plane and then move to another plane.

83
00:06:28.199 --> 00:06:38.199
Come forward towards the camera, grab three to five points in that view, and then move the tracked object back a little bit further and grab five points from there as well.

84
00:06:38.199 --> 00:06:45.199
That's going to give us the option so that when we do add this nodal point calibration, we're going to get an RMS number that we're going to try and achieve.

85
00:06:45.199 --> 00:06:50.199
And that RMS number should be below 0.1, so 0.1 or better.

86
00:06:50.199 --> 00:06:57.199
So again, let's go ahead and hit our add to nodal offset calibration, and that's going to give us our RMS reprojection error.

87
00:06:57.199 --> 00:07:00.199
And our error in this case is zero, which is perfect.

88
00:07:00.199 --> 00:07:05.199
So since we've got this nice error here, we're going to go ahead and click OK and apply.

89
00:07:05.199 --> 00:07:12.199
And now when I go ahead and take a look at my transparency, if I scrub through, you can see that it's going to align a lot better.

90
00:07:12.199 --> 00:07:14.199
So there's no more overlay offset here.

91
00:07:14.199 --> 00:07:21.199
Now, to verify that, I am just going to scroll in and zoom in really quickly here, and you can see how that alignment is there.

92
00:07:21.199 --> 00:07:30.199
Now, if I go ahead and have Sean run over to the puck really quickly, we will be able to see if the ghost Sean over there is going to start to move it.

93
00:07:30.199 --> 00:07:32.199
So he just grabs the entire thing and moves it somewhere.

94
00:07:32.199 --> 00:07:33.199
It doesn't matter where.

95
00:07:33.199 --> 00:07:38.199
And then right there, as he moves it, it just follows and lines up back into place.

96
00:07:38.199 --> 00:07:46.199
That tells me that we're actually tracking the object, both in the engine and on the device here, and that the alignment is kind of right where we want it to be.

97
00:07:46.199 --> 00:07:51.199
It also helps by having this sticker realign so that the center of that point here also aligns that way.

98
00:07:51.199 --> 00:07:58.199
Now, really quick, let's go ahead and jump into our slides and review really quickly the nodal point offset calibration.

99
00:07:58.199 --> 00:08:03.199
And I'll point out that this is a separate calibration step that has to be done for your virtual camera transforms,

100
00:08:03.199 --> 00:08:09.199
and it will continue being set by your nodal point of the physical lens as the tracked camera moves within your stage volume.

101
00:08:09.199 --> 00:08:14.199
Now, as I showed you just a moment ago, the offset application in here, just to verify it,

102
00:08:14.199 --> 00:08:17.199
you just change your transparency from 0 to 0.5.

103
00:08:17.199 --> 00:08:21.199
You move your OptiPak track object, whatever your tracked object is, around.

104
00:08:21.199 --> 00:08:26.199
It should track and superimpose itself correctly over the physical object.

105
00:08:26.199 --> 00:08:31.199
If not, you can go ahead and remove this nodal point lens file and replace it and do it again.

106
00:08:31.199 --> 00:08:35.200
Just get a few more points to calibrate this time, and you should be good to go.

107
00:08:35.200 --> 00:08:38.200
Now, one thing to note before we finish up here,

108
00:08:38.200 --> 00:08:43.200
if you did not set up time code in Genlock and adjusted the synchronations using the time data monitor,

109
00:08:43.200 --> 00:08:46.200
there might be lag in your CG element as you move.

110
00:08:46.200 --> 00:08:50.200
There should always be a little bit, but when you stop moving, even if there is any kind of lag,

111
00:08:50.200 --> 00:08:53.200
your CG element will line up again if it's done correctly.

112
00:08:53.200 --> 00:08:56.200
Okay, so that's it for your nodal point calibration.

113
00:08:56.200 --> 00:08:59.200
Now we are going to move on to your LED wall alignment,

114
00:08:59.200 --> 00:09:03.200
and we'll finish up that next section after we build our Arrucos markers.
